<!DOCTYPE html>
<!--
  **The doctype has to be the first thing in the file, and it is load-bearing rather than tidy.**
  Installer sniffs the pane's *data* to decide whether it is HTML or plain text, and a file starting
  with a comment fails that sniff -- so the whole page is shown as raw markup, this comment included.
  `mime-type="text/html"` in the Distribution does not rescue it, and
  tools/platform/macos/installer-remote.sh asserts the first line on every build.

  The pane Installer shows first. The placeholder below is the only substitution, and the snippet
  that fills it comes from tools/platform/macos/pkg/ unchanged: Gatekeeper's obstacle is a property
  of the archive rather than of the product, so the two carriers have one copy of that text between
  them. **Naming the placeholder here would be a second mention**, and the renderer refuses anything
  but exactly one -- every mention is substituted, including one inside a comment.

  **A separate file from the all-in-one's, rather than the same one with a condition in it.** That
  one names four applications, /usr/local/bin and a packages folder, and every sentence of it would
  need a qualifier here. A pane with conditionals in it is how a pane comes to say something untrue.
-->
<html>
<head><meta charset="utf-8"></head>
<body style="font: 13px -apple-system, sans-serif; margin: 0">

<p><b>KM Remote</b> &mdash; the karaoke remote, with your own copy of the song list. Point it at a
karaoke machine once and it copies the whole catalog onto this Mac, so browsing, searching and your
favorites work whether or not the machine is switched on.</p>

<p>This installs the application alone. Nothing is placed on your <code>PATH</code> and no file type
is claimed. The karaoke machine itself is a separate download, which carries this program too.</p>

@SIGNING@

<h4>Removing it later</h4>

<p>Open <b>/usr/local/km-remote</b> and double-click <b>Uninstall KM Remote</b>. It shows you what it
is about to remove, asks, and then asks for your password &mdash; in that order. From a terminal,
<code>sudo /usr/local/km-remote/uninstall.sh</code> does the same thing without the questions.</p>

<p>Either way it leaves your copy of the song list and your favorites alone, and prints where they
are on its way out.</p>

</body>
</html>
